A||||4||gtsonly||spacers for split plenum||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||09-16-2000||06:20 AM||||Let me ask the question, would there be or not, any benefit to increasing the distance, by adding a 1/2" or 1" milled spacer between the split air plenum (if I am calling it by it's right name, the air plenum or intake connected to the TB)?  On my old mustang 5.0, I had a 2 1/2" spacer between the carb and the manifold to create more low end torgue and HP. The spacer allows incoming gas and air to mix throughly and cool prior to being induced in to the combustion chamber. If threoy works, by adding a spacer between the split air plenum/intake manifold would create the same. anyone out there got any thoughts?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPS<BR>http://www.fiero.nl/cgi-bin/fiero/ubbmisc.cgi||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||09-16-2000||01:41 PM||||Since you asked for thoughts, here're a few obvious ones:<P>1)Since the stock Fiero is port fuel injected, there's no mixing going on in the area you're talking about adding a spacer.  Therefore, the "better mixing of fuel/air" probably won't happen.<P>2)I have the notion that relatively long, skinny intake runners help low-end torque by increasing average velocity of the stuff flowing into the ports - the momentum of the A/F mixture producing a slight "ram" effect. <P>3) The only way to really know about something is to try it.<P>4)If you decide to try it just to see what happens, be sure to measure how much clearance you've got between the top of the plenum and the underside of the hood.  Keep in mind that the engine moves around in there ("Dude-! This engine <B>rocks</B>!)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">      <P>4) Lastly, and most importantly, I probably don't know what I'm talking about    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ! <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 09-16-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||StuGood||09-16-2000||02:33 PM||||I re-read your post. Guess I'm still not quite clear where you are thinking of putting spacer(s)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> .  In any case:<P>If you're talking about putting (2) spacers between the upper plenum and (what I call) the intermediate manifold, what I said still applies.<P>If you're thinking of putting a spacer plate bewteen the intermediate manifold and the lower intake manifold, you won't be able to plug the injectors into their ports, because the fuel rail will be lifted up.  ||||reg||4
Z||000003||rockcrawl||09-16-2000||08:17 PM||||A phenolic spacer may keep the upper intake cooler, thus the incoming air would be cooler, and we all know that cooler air is better. But then there's that problem with the injectors not fitting back in place! Like StuGood said, the fuel doesn't even enter the system until the lower intake runners. A throttle body spacer on a 2.5 would probably be more feasible, and far more effective.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||09-16-2000||09:00 PM||||I have 1 inch phenolic sheet at work. It will probably be to thick for deck lid clearence. But if you want to cool the throttle body just do the bypass of the water lines.||||reg||1
